<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="biostats-notes-5-1-goodness-of-fit" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Goodness of Fit </title>
    
    <objectives>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Students will be able to...
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <ol>
            <li><p> Conduct goodness of fit tests. </p></li>
        </ol>
    </objectives>

    <definition xml:id="def-goodness-of-fit">
        <statement>
            <p>
                The measurement of how well a proposed probability distribution describes a population is called the <alert> goodness-of-fit </alert> of the distribution. It is computed 
                <md>
                    \Delta p = \sum_{k} \frac{(p_k - \hat{p}_k)^2}{p_k} = \sum_{\text{all proportions}} \frac{\text{difference}^2}{\text{expected}}
                </md>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-goodness-of-fit">
        <statement>
            <p>
                The goodness of fit between two probability distributions follows a <alert><m>\chi^2</m>-distribution</alert> with <m>k - 1</m> degrees of freedom, where <m>k</m> is the number of proportions in the distribution. It has mean <m>k-1</m> and standard deviation <m>\sqrt{2(k-1)}</m>. We write <md> \Delta p \sim \chi^2_{k-1}.</md> 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <p>
        Draw a picture of the distribution. Point out it starts from 0 and is heavily skewed right. As <m>k \to \infty</m>, <m>\chi^2 \to \cal{N}</m>. 
    </p>

    <example>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                A commercial florist claims their assorted roses follow the below distribution. 
            </p>
            <tabular>
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell>Color</cell> <cell>Proportion</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Red</cell><cell>58%</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>White</cell><cell>32%</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Pink</cell><cell>10%</cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
            <p>
                In an order of 68 flowers, a customer received 
            </p>
            <tabular>
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell>Color</cell> <cell>Count</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Red</cell><cell>38</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>White</cell><cell>24</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Pink</cell><cell>6</cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
            
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The customer complains that their order did not have the distribution of colors they expected. State the null and alternative hypotheses.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    We have 
                    <md>
                        <mrow> H_0: \amp p_{\text{red}} = 0.58 \wedge p_{\text{white}} = 0.32 \wedge p_{\text{pink}} = 0.1 </mrow>
                        <mrow> H_1: \amp p_{\text{red}} \neq 0.58 \vee p_{\text{white}} \neq 0.32 \vee p_{\text{pink}} \neq 0.1 </mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Measure the goodness-of-fit between the customer's order and the florist's claim.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    According to the florist, had we ordered 68 flowers, we would have expected to receive 34.8 red, 21.8 white, and 6.8 pink flowers. Hence, the goodness-of-fit is 
                    <md>
                        \Delta p = \frac{(34.8-38)^2}{34.8} + \frac{(21.8-24)^2}{21.8} + \frac{(6.8-6)^2}{6.8} \approx 0.62.
                    </md>
                    
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use the customer's order to test the florist's claim with 5% significance. 
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Start by drawing the picture. The idea is the same for other hypothesis tests. All goodness-of-fit tests are one-tailed, since we are essentially looking for <q>too large of an error</q>. Since we have 3 colors, we have 2 degrees of freedom. We compute <md> \chi^{2*}_2 \approx 5.99 </md> using the R command <c>qchisq(0.95,2)</c>. Notice that our goodness of fit is acceptable. There is insignificant evidence to reject <m>H_0</m>. 
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        
    </example>

    
    

</section>
